WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.672
Narrator: For those of you who sat through

00:00:07.672 --> 00:00:10.695
"Avengers: Endgame" and
expected two post-credit scenes,

00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:12.329
you will be really pleased to hear

00:00:12.329 --> 00:00:13.903
that "Spider-Man: Far From Home"

00:00:13.903 --> 00:00:15.840
does actually have two of them.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.870
Now, these set up

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.530
for further Marvel Cinematic
Universe characters

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.100
as well as giving a nod
back to "Captain Marvel."

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.100
We're gonna take a look at both of them.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.683
And, yes, spoilers are ahead.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.350
In the first post-credit scene,

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.610
Peter, as Spider-Man,
and MJ are now dating.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:41.740
She knows his identity, but
it is still a wider secret.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.610
The pair swing into New
York West 33rd Street

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.310
and Eighth Avenue.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:47.930
A breaking-news sting hits a screen

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.250
above New York Penn Station.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.450
Mysterio, played by Jake Gyllenhaal,

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.330
was killed in the final battle

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.470
but is seen giving a
manipulated, prerecorded message

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:00.750
that a drone strike on Tower
Bridge was Spider-Man's fault

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.560
and that Peter Parker is Spider-Man.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.400
The screen then cuts to J.
Jonah Jameson on The Daily Bugle

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.813
repeating this exposé.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.810
Fans of the Sam Raimi-era
"Spider-Man" franchise

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.930
will be pleased that J.K. Simmons returns

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.193
as J. Jonah Jameson.

00:01:17.193 --> 00:01:20.077
He also appeared as the
character in "Avengers Assemble,"

00:01:20.077 --> 00:01:22.553
"Ultimate Spider-Man," and other titles.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.430
So the most vital thing
that we can take away

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.630
from this exposé is that either,

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.660
for future Spidey installments,

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.240
Peter won't be living his double life

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.890
or they'll have to explain
this incident away as a lie.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.230
It also showcases the power of the media

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.780
to manipulate the superhero
image in a post-"Endgame" MCU.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.260
It's also rather prescient
in the era of fake news.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.607
After all, Mysterio's final
lines on Tower Bridge were,

00:01:49.607 --> 00:01:52.207
"People just need to
believe, and, nowadays,

00:01:52.207 --> 00:01:53.657
they will believe anything."

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.580
We previously sat through the whole

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.280
Spidey-as-the-bad-guy scenario
in the Sam Raimi trilogy,

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.193
so is the franchise really
going to set this up again?

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.153
Captain Marvel: I'm gonna end it.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.580
Narrator: In the second
post-credit scene, Nick Fury

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.350
and Agent Maria Hill are seen
driving down a city street

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.290
talking about how
Quentin/Mysterio fooled them.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.794
Then, in a huge twist,
Fury transforms back into

00:02:21.794 --> 00:02:24.820
the Skrull Talos, Ben Mendelsohn,

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.660
as Talos had been impersonating Fury

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.338
throughout the whole entire movie.

00:02:29.338 --> 00:02:32.030
Fans will remember him as one
of the initial antagonists

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.770
in "Captain Marvel," who
had been impersonating Fury

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.773
early in the film, then was
revealed to be a good guy.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.320
Soren, who had been impersonating Hill,

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.310
tells Talos that they
need to call Nick Fury

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.153
to update him on what's happened.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.370
Talos calls the real Nick Fury.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.720
We initially believe him
to be on a tropical island,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.820
barefoot, drinking out of a coconut,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.080
and wearing a tropical shirt.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.210
But the island scene is a
hologram, and Fury is actually

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:02.850
on a large Skrull ship
traveling through space.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.630
He's relaxing and doesn't seem
to be in any present danger.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.440
So the first question: When
did Talos become Fury on Earth?

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.000
Did Fury come back
immediately after the snap,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.090
then have something
important to attend to,

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:18.730
so sent Talos to impersonate him?

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:22.100
If, in the movie, Talos
remembers Tony Stark's funeral,

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.470
then it is possible
that the real Nick Fury

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.620
didn't actually attend.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.800
Perhaps he is so relaxed
on the ship, though,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.610
because they're waiting
for something to happen

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.950
or are making a very long journey.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.733
We know Captain Marvel is missing

00:03:34.733 --> 00:03:36.683
from chunks of "Avengers: Endgame"

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:37.720
because she was off helping

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.910
other planets affected by the snap.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:43.323
Is Fury assisting with
this or on his own quest?

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.530
Looking back at Fury's lines in the film,

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.870
the audience is dropped a few small clues

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.763
that he was a Skrull all along.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.400
When asked whether Captain
Marvel is available

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.917
to help fight the Elementals, Fury says,

00:03:56.917 --> 00:03:59.140
"Don't invoke her name," which seems like

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.910
a bit of an overreaction.

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.940
Also, at the end of the
movie, he tells Happy Hogan

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:05.833
that appearances can be deceiving.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.640
Fury's appearance on the Skrull
ship is a potential setup

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.823
for the next MCU installments.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:15.640
We already know that the
Skrulls of the movies

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.310
divert from their character
arc in the comics.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.060
They appear as antagonists
to the Fantastic Four

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.510
in 1962's "Fantastic Four No. 2,"

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:28.210
and then fighting the
Kree-Skrull war in "Avengers,"

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.160
and then take over human form on Earth

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.557
in 2008's "Secret Invasion."

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.150
And at the end of "Captain
Marvel," they're portrayed

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.890
as peaceful refugees that
are aligned to Captain Marvel

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.690
and follow her through the universe.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.720
We've also had a brief crossover
into "Guardians" before.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.460
In 2014's "Guardians of the Galaxy,"

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:51.050
during Stan Lee's cameo,
Rocket is scanning

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.470
members of the public from a long range.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:56.960
The equipment shows Skrull
writing up on the screen.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.400
So the characters from "Guardians"

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.030
might have had some familiarity
with the Skrulls before.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:06.060
Disney has eight untitled Marvel
movies slated through 2022,

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.300
so it's possible that
Fury's journey will overlap.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.380
The next logical fit for Fury
and the Skrulls off-world

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.030
might be the reportedly cosmic setting

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.610
of "Guardians of the Galaxy
3" or "Captain Marvel 2."

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:21.440
There's also "The Eternals" in development

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.230
as well as a reported
"Fantastic Four" reboot

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:25.833
as early as 2022.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.940
Considering the Skrulls
were first featured

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:32.080
in the "Fantastic Four" comics,
this would be a great way

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.610
to tie together two elements of the MCU.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.150
What did you think of the
two post-credit scenes?

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:38.983
Comment below.

